#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070021 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the point of intersection between the two lines $y = x + 2$ and $y = 2x + 1.$ A sketch of both lines and the point of intersection $P$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bf429fdfbe706521b81617f56a0f561171aa7fd48e0af89318225f1d328e667.png)\n\nThe point $P$ consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=x+2$ and $y=2x+1.$ So, to find the coordinates of $P$ we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=x+2$ into the second equation $y=2x+1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =2x \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1, \\, y=3.$ So, the point of intersection has the coordinates $(1,3).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39070934-9f3c-5858-8959-365242cf0563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070034 :tutorial/content "When two lines are given in the *standard form*, you may find it easier to use the *elimination method* to find the point of intersection.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "efdf9704-19aa-5a4a-8ac3-220f267bc227" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9140 :tutorial/title "Lines Given in Standard Form"}]}
